<title>GERMANY: Tietmeyer says sees no creative accounting by Bonn.</title>
<headline>Tietmeyer says sees no creative accounting by Bonn.</headline>
<dateline>FRANKFURT 1997-04-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Bundesbank president Hans Tietmeyer said on Thursday that there were no signs the Bonn government was resorting to creative accounting methods to reach entry goals for Europe's planned currency union.</p>
<p>At a news conference Tietmeyer said he had not seen signs of creative accounting being used in Bonn and added &quot;I wish that were the case in all European Union nations.&quot;</p>
<p>Germany has in the past been a very vocal opponent of certain one-off measures that could help nations meet the strict budget target of three percent of gross domestic product.</p>
<p>Both France and Italy have introduced certain measures to help trim their budget deficits.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
</text>
